.S has kept a  network that allows American troops to  mobilize quickly  for instance following the September  11th attacks the U.S took over Camp  lemonier from France and it became a key  facility for American troops to fight  against al-shabab al qaeda's affiliate  in Somalia in 2018 the Pentagon said the  U.S had more than 500 bases around the  world though some military researchers  and experts say there could be more  China on the other hand doesn't have a  constellation of military bases because  for a long time the leadership viewed it  as a sign of imperialism there had been  Decades of official declaratory policy  from China that they would never station  forces overseas Isaac Cardin is an  assistant professor at the U.S naval war  college and he's been studying China's  Maritime Affairs for more than 10 years  they were attempting to position  themselves as partners of the developing  World specifically against the Americans  who were building bases the beijing's  policies began to shift in the early  2010s as China's economic footprint  began to grow in the first decade of  this Century the country's share in  global trade has almost tripled the  leadership in Beijing recognized that  the scope and intensity of China's  interests overseas required some more  military capability one of those places  was in the Gulf of Aden where the  whether piracy posed threats to global  Shipping this is a route that China  relies on to ship goods between the  mainland and the Mediterranean  so in 2016 in an effort to counter  piracy Beijing said it would set up in  Djibouti what it called an army support  base Djibouti is probably the most  effective place for the people's  Liberation Army to start standing itself  up outside of China's immediate region  given the mission to facilitate sea  operations the Chinese base is equipped  differently from Camp lemonier its first  and foremost a naval base so China built  a Pier from scratch military experts say  it's large enough to host various  vessels in its Fleet including aircraft  carriers destroyers and submarines  the site is about 90 acres and Military  experts estimate it can host about 2 000  Personnel just compare that with Camp  lemonier the American Site is  approximately five times larger and can  house about double the personnel it not  only has Naval support but also land and  air forces that can participate in  military exercises and conflicts and the  bigger size also means the U.S spends  more on paying for the location than  China it pays the djiboutian government  about 63 million dollars annually to  rent the place according to an  independent security Think Tank while  China hasn't disclosed Financial details  a report by the U.S congressional  research service says it costs about 20  million dollars per year to lease the  site  besides Camp lemonier the U.S has even  bigger overseas bases that can house  more than 30 thousand people and they're  usually called Little America it has at  least four schools a hospital  fast food facilities a giant mall and  all the trappings of a significant U.S  town or city  China's single base falls short in terms  of the size and scope of what the U.S  has but Beijing also uses a system of  more than 90 commercial ports and  terminals outside of China  It's Not Unusual for countries including  the US to use them to dock Naval vessels  for repairs and to resupply what is  unusual is that China is able to use  commercial facilities that are owned and  operated  mostly by state-owned Enterprises so the  pla has been granted some type of  preferential access to it Cardin says  most of the ports are partially  controlled by Chinese firms meaning they  own or operate one or more terminals but  all this can still provide a unique  Advantage China is amassing incredible  stockpiles of data about global trade on  the basis of its position in commercial  ports worldwide  Cardin says this Insight alongside a  military presence can be a powerful  combination that could challenge the US  and its allies when you consider that  China is potentially in a position to  deny or delay or disrupt or even control  vital shipments so ultimately this  network puts them in a position where  they have some coercive Leverage  for now military experts say China isn't  pursuing an extensive network like the  US the concerns have been growing among  some American officials that Beijing  could be planning new bases they are  seeking actively seeking a military base  on the Atlantic coast of Africa the  place where they've actually made  progress toward this is Equatorial  Guinea  China's defense ministry didn't respond  to a request for comment on its base  plans in Africa or whether Beijing could  use commercial ports to counter other  countries  the U.S defense department said it's  concerned about the lack of transparency  and Clarity around beijing's  negotiations with host Nations  so as the U.S and China compete for  Global influence the networks that they  build and maintain could become one of  their most essential military assets  [Music]

